insert into brainilab_editor.articles(slug,document) values('how-to-read-roman-numerals','{"slug": "how-to-read-roman-numerals", "status": "published", "topic": "History", "title": "Roman numerals: read the small pieces first", "description": "Turn a string like MCMXLIV into manageable parts, with a short practice set to check yourself.", "game": {"name": "History Quiz", "url": "/history/history-quiz/"}, "hub": {"name": "History", "url": "/history/"}, "sections": [{"id": "start-seven", "title": "Seven symbols are enough to begin", "html": "<p>A date carved over a doorway can look forbidding until you stop reading it as one long word. Roman numerals use a small set of symbols. For the common forms you meet in quizzes, start here:</p><p class=\"number-example\">I = 1 · V = 5 · X = 10 · L = 50<br/>C = 100 · D = 500 · M = 1,000</p><p>Read VI as five plus one: six. Read VIII as five plus three: eight. Build a little confidence with those before tackling a building date.</p>"}, {"id": "pairs", "title": "Look for the subtraction pairs", "html": "<p>In standard modern notation, a smaller symbol before an appropriate larger one can form a subtraction pair. The familiar pairs are IV (4), IX (9), XL (40), XC (90), CD (400) and CM (900).</p><p>This is not permission to put any small symbol before any large one. Write 49 as XLIX: forty plus nine. IL is not the standard form. Historical inscriptions and clock faces can use variants, including IIII for four, so a real object may not follow a school exercise exactly.</p>"}, {"id": "doorway-date", "title": "A doorway date, one chunk at a time", "html": "<p>Try MCMXLIV. Separate it into M | CM | XL | IV. You now have 1,000 + 900 + 40 + 4, which gives 1944. Each chunk is easier to check than a hurried running total.</p><p>For a second pass, go backwards: build 1944 from thousands, hundreds, tens and units. If the pieces match, you have checked the reading without merely repeating the same mental step.</p><details class=\"practice-answer\"><summary>What is MMXXVI?</summary><p>2026: MM is 2,000, XX is 20 and VI is 6. There are no subtraction pairs in this example.</p></details>"}, {"id": "practice", "title": "Three small checks", "html": "<p>On paper, try XIV, XLII and XC. Before opening the answers, circle any subtraction pair. Then read the remaining symbols normally.</p><details class=\"practice-answer\"><summary>Show the three answers</summary><p>XIV = 14; XLII = 42; XC = 90. In XIV, only IV is the subtraction pair: the initial X still contributes ten.</p></details><p>Next time a quiz includes a Roman numeral, look for a familiar pair first. You do not need to hold the whole string in memory.</p>"}], "sources": [{"name": "Wolfram MathWorld — Roman numerals", "url": "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/RomanNumerals.html"}, {"name": "University of Cambridge NRICH — Roman numerals", "url": "https://nrich.maths.org/problems/roman-numerals?tab=teacher"}], "related": ["which-century-is-that-year", "number-sequences-look-at-the-gaps"], "practice": "Try History Quiz. For dates written in Roman numerals, split the symbols into chunks before choosing an answer.", "cover": {"src": "/assets/images/learn/how-to-read-roman-numerals.webp", "alt": "A stone clock face with Roman numerals", "credit": "AI-generated cover illustration created for BrainiLab."}, "order": 10, "publishedAt": "2026-09-23T19:51:37Z"}'::jsonb) on conflict do nothing;
