insert into brainilab_editor.articles(slug,document) values('why-time-zones-get-messy','{"slug": "why-time-zones-get-messy", "status": "published", "topic": "Geography", "title": "Why time zones don’t follow tidy lines", "description": "Earth turns steadily. Our clocks follow borders, shared routines and decisions that can change.", "game": {"name": "World Capitals Quiz", "url": "/geography/world-capitals-quiz/"}, "hub": {"name": "Geography", "url": "/geography/"}, "sections": [{"id": "simple-idea", "title": "A simple idea meets a complicated world", "html": "<p>You arrange a call with a friend abroad, confidently add an hour, and still get it wrong. The arithmetic may be fine. The assumption behind it is usually the problem.</p><p>As Earth rotates, different places face the Sun at different times. Dividing 360 degrees by 24 hours gives 15 degrees per hour: a useful starting picture. Civil time, however, is set by people. Time-zone borders and clock changes do not have to follow evenly spaced lines of longitude.</p>"}, {"id": "offset-is-not-zone", "title": "A UTC offset is not the whole story", "html": "<p>An offset tells you how far a clock is ahead of or behind UTC at a particular moment. A time zone also contains rules about when that offset changes. IANA’s time-zone database is updated to reflect political decisions about boundaries, offsets and daylight-saving rules.</p><p>That is why ‘five hours behind me’ is a fragile contact note. Save a city and a date when planning a meeting. A calendar that knows both locations can account for the applicable rules. Do not assume that two places change their clocks on the same weekend.</p>"}, {"id": "clock-arithmetic", "title": "Practise with fixed offsets first", "html": "<p>Here is a made-up scheduling example with the offsets already supplied. City A is UTC+2 and City B is UTC−3. If it is 14:00 in A, subtract two hours to find UTC: 12:00. Then subtract three more for B: 09:00.</p><p>Writing the intermediate UTC step is less error-prone than juggling ‘ahead’ and ‘behind’ in your head. Near midnight, write the date too.</p><details class=\"practice-answer\"><summary>It is 01:00 on Tuesday at UTC+2. What time is it at UTC−3?</summary><p>20:00 on Monday. First go back two hours to 23:00 Monday UTC, then back three more hours.</p></details>"}, {"id": "quiz-clue", "title": "Keep geography and clock rules separate", "html": "<p>Knowing where a city is helps you picture the journey, but longitude alone does not tell you its current legal time. In a quiz, read whether the question supplies an offset or asks about a specific place and date. Those are different kinds of problem.</p>"}], "sources": [{"name": "IANA — Time-zone database and civil-time changes", "url": "https://www.iana.org/time-zones/tz-link"}, {"name": "Royal Museums Greenwich — Local time and the railways", "url": "https://www.rmg.co.uk/stories/time/time-greenwich-mean-time-confusing-case-travellers-watch"}], "related": ["why-greenland-looks-so-big", "mental-percentages-without-a-calculator"], "practice": "Explore World Capitals to connect city names with places. For an actual call or journey, check the date-specific local time.", "cover": {"src": "/assets/images/learn/why-time-zones-get-messy.webp", "alt": "Three clocks showing different times", "credit": "AI-generated cover illustration created for BrainiLab."}, "order": 11, "publishedAt": "2026-09-23T19:51:37Z"}'::jsonb) on conflict do nothing;
